-- Q1: easy / L1 — Standard deduction add-back for AMT
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'For AMT purposes, what happens to the standard deduction claimed on a taxpayer''s regular tax return?', '["It is added back to taxable income as an AMT adjustment", "It is allowed as a deduction for both regular tax and AMT", "It is replaced by a separate AMT standard deduction amount", "It is reduced by 25% for AMT purposes"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 56(b)(1)(E), the standard deduction is fully added back to taxable income when computing AMTI because the AMT system does not permit the standard deduction. Taxpayers who claim the standard deduction must add the entire amount back as an adjustment. Choice B is incorrect because the AMT disallows the standard deduction entirely; there is no corresponding AMT standard deduction.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — SALT add-back AMTI calculation
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Delgado, a single filer, reports regular taxable income of $280,000 for 2026. His itemized deductions included $10,000 in state and local taxes (the SALT cap), $18,000 in mortgage interest on acquisition indebtedness, and $6,000 in charitable contributions. For AMT purposes, the SALT deduction is added back. What is Delgado''s AMTI before the AMT exemption?', '["$280,000", "$290,000", "$314,000", "$296,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 56(b)(1)(A), state and local tax deductions are added back to taxable income for AMT purposes. Delgado''s AMTI is $280,000 + $10,000 SALT = $290,000. Mortgage interest on acquisition debt and charitable contributions are allowed under both systems. Choice C ($314,000) is incorrect because it adds back all itemized deductions, whereas only SALT is disallowed for AMT.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: hard / L4 — Evaluate ISO exercise timing and AMT planning
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Nakamura, a single filer with regular taxable income of $400,000, plans to exercise ISOs with a total spread of $150,000 in December 2026. Her advisor estimates regular tax at $95,000 and TMT without the ISO at $88,000. If she exercises, AMTI increases by $150,000. The AMT exemption for singles is $88,100 (phasing out at $578,150). Evaluate the most appropriate advice regarding ISO exercise timing.', '["Exercise all options in December because the MTC will fully offset any AMT the following year", "Exercise all options because the $150,000 spread is below the AMT exemption amount", "Consider splitting the exercise across two tax years to minimize cumulative AMT exposure", "Delay the entire exercise indefinitely because AMT cannot apply to incentive stock options"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 56(b)(3), the ISO bargain element is an AMT adjustment in the exercise year. Splitting exercises across tax years reduces peak AMTI, keeping more income within the 26% bracket and preserving exemption amounts in each year. Choice A is incorrect because the MTC only recovers prior AMT to the extent regular tax exceeds TMT in future years, which is not guaranteed. Choice D is wrong because ISOs are a well-established AMT trigger.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Capital gains preferential rate under AMT
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Under the AMT system, how are net long-term capital gains taxed for individual taxpayers?', '["At the regular AMT rates of 26% and 28%", "At a flat 28% AMT capital gains rate", "At ordinary income rates because AMT disallows preferential treatment", "At the same preferential rates that apply for regular tax purposes"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 55(b)(3), the AMT computation applies the same preferential capital gains tax rates (0%, 15%, or 20%) used for regular tax purposes. Net capital gains are not subjected to the 26%/28% AMT rates. Choice A is incorrect because subjecting capital gains to the standard AMT rates would overstate the tax; the Code specifically preserves the lower rates within the AMT calculation.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — MFS AMT exemption phase-out calculation
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Fielding and Graves are married but file separate returns for 2026. Fielding has AMTI of $700,000 before the exemption. The MFS AMT exemption is $68,500, and the phase-out begins at $609,000 at a rate of 25 cents per dollar. What is Fielding''s reduced AMT exemption?', '["$45,750 after partial phase-out", "$68,500 with no phase-out reduction", "$0 because the exemption is fully phased out", "$22,750 as the phase-out reduction amount"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 55(d), the MFS AMT exemption of $68,500 is reduced by 25% of AMTI exceeding $609,000. Fielding''s excess is $700,000 − $609,000 = $91,000; 25% of $91,000 = $22,750. The reduced exemption is $68,500 − $22,750 = $45,750. Choice B ($68,500) is incorrect because it assumes no phase-out applies, whereas AMTI above $609,000 triggers a reduction. Choice D confuses the reduction amount with the remaining exemption.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — ATNOL limitation effect on AMT
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Quintero Corporation has AMTI of $800,000 before any net operating loss deduction for 2026. It has an alternative tax net operating loss (ATNOL) carryforward of $900,000. Under current NOL rules, the ATNOL deduction is limited to 80% of AMTI. Analyze the effect of the ATNOL limitation on Quintero''s final AMTI.', '["The ATNOL is capped at $640,000, leaving AMTI of $160,000 subject to AMT rates", "The full $900,000 ATNOL offsets AMTI, reducing it to zero", "The ATNOL deduction is limited to $480,000, leaving $320,000 subject to AMT", "NOL carryforwards are disallowed entirely for AMT purposes"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 56(d), the alternative tax NOL deduction cannot exceed 80% of AMTI computed without the ATNOL. Here, 80% of $800,000 = $640,000, which caps the deduction. AMTI after the ATNOL is $800,000 − $640,000 = $160,000. The remaining $260,000 unused ATNOL carries forward. Choice B is incorrect because post-2017 NOLs cannot reduce AMTI below 20% of pre-ATNOL AMTI.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: easy / L1 — Form 6251 filing requirement
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Which IRS form is used by individual taxpayers to calculate the alternative minimum tax?', '["Form 1040 Schedule D", "Form 6251", "Form 4952", "Form 8801"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 55, individual taxpayers compute the AMT on Form 6251, Alternative Minimum Tax—Individuals. This form calculates AMTI, applies the exemption, and determines whether AMT is owed. Choice D (Form 8801) is incorrect because it is the Credit for Prior Year Minimum Tax, not the AMT computation form. Choice C (Form 4952) relates to investment interest expense deductions.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — AMT foreign tax credit interaction
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Chambers, a single filer, owes $18,000 in AMT for 2026. She paid $25,000 in foreign income taxes. Her regular tax FTC limitation is $30,000, but her separate AMT foreign tax credit limitation is $15,000. Analyze how the foreign tax credit interacts with Chambers''s AMT liability.', '["The FTC eliminates the entire $18,000 AMT because foreign taxes paid exceed the AMT amount", "The FTC does not apply against AMT and only reduces regular tax liability", "The AMT FTC is capped at $15,000, offsetting that amount against the $18,000 AMT", "The full $25,000 in foreign taxes paid offsets the AMT dollar for dollar"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 59(a), the AMT foreign tax credit is computed separately from the regular tax FTC using its own limitation formula. The AMT FTC is capped at $15,000, so only $15,000 of the $18,000 AMT is offset, leaving $3,000 in AMT. Choice A is incorrect because the AMT FTC has its own separate limitation that must be independently computed and applied.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: hard / L4 — Multi-factor evaluation: AMT planning with ISO spread allocation
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Torres, a single filer with regular taxable income of $500,000, holds ISOs with a total spread of $300,000. Her advisor models two scenarios: exercising all ISOs in Year 1, or $150,000 in each of Year 1 and Year 2. The AMT exemption is $88,100, phasing out at $578,150. Torres expects consistent income in both years. Evaluate which strategy minimizes combined AMT over two years.', '["Both strategies produce identical total AMT because the aggregate spread is the same", "Exercising all in Year 1 is preferable because it consolidates the MTC carryforward", "The combined AMT cannot be determined without knowing Torres''s state tax bracket", "Splitting the exercise across two years minimizes total AMT by preserving more exemption and keeping income in the 26% bracket each year"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 55(b), the AMT uses graduated 26%/28% rates with a breakpoint near $232,600 above the exemption. Concentrating the full $300,000 spread in one year pushes AMTI further into the 28% bracket and accelerates exemption phase-out. Splitting preserves exemption in both years and keeps more income at 26%. Choice A is incorrect because the progressive rate structure and phase-out create a convex tax function where splitting income reduces total tax.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — Medical expense threshold alignment for AMT
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Prescott, age 68, has AGI of $120,000 and $15,000 in unreimbursed medical expenses for 2026. She itemizes deductions. For regular tax purposes, medical expenses exceeding 7.5% of AGI are deductible. Analyze how Prescott''s medical expense deduction is treated for AMT purposes under current law.', '["The full $15,000 is deductible for AMT because medical expenses have no floor under AMT", "Medical expenses are entirely disallowed for AMT, requiring the full deduction to be added back", "The AMT applies a higher 10% AGI floor, reducing Prescott''s AMT medical deduction to $3,000", "The same 7.5% AGI floor applies for both regular tax and AMT, so the deduction is identical under both systems"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 56(b)(1)(B), as amended by the TCJA, the medical expense floor for AMT is the same 7.5% of AGI used for regular tax. Prescott deducts $15,000 − ($120,000 × 7.5%) = $6,000 under both systems, creating no AMT adjustment. Choice C is incorrect because the previously higher 10% AMT threshold was eliminated; the 7.5% floor now applies uniformly.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: hard / L4 — ISO basis difference: AMT vs regular tax on later sale
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Walsh exercised ISOs in Year 1, acquiring 2,000 shares at $25 per share when FMV was $60 per share, paying $14,000 in AMT on the $70,000 spread. In Year 2, Walsh sells all shares for $75 per share. Evaluate Walsh''s AMT basis and the difference between regular tax and AMT capital gain on the sale.', '["AMT basis is $120,000; regular gain is $100,000 while AMT gain is $30,000, creating a $70,000 difference that generates MTC recovery", "AMT basis equals the $50,000 exercise price; both regular and AMT gain are $100,000", "AMT basis is $120,000 and AMT gain is $30,000, but the $14,000 prior AMT is automatically refunded", "AMT basis is $150,000 (sale price); no AMT gain is recognized on the sale"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 56(b)(3), the ISO exercise creates an AMT adjustment equal to the spread, giving Walsh an AMT basis of $60 × 2,000 = $120,000 versus a regular tax basis of $25 × 2,000 = $50,000. On sale at $75/share ($150,000), regular gain is $100,000 while AMT gain is only $30,000. The $70,000 difference generates a minimum tax credit under Section 53. Choice B is incorrect because it uses the exercise price as AMT basis, whereas the AMT basis includes the previously taxed spread.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q12: hard / L4 — AMT and passive activity loss recomputation
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Dunn actively participates in a rental activity and has $40,000 in passive rental losses for 2026. For regular tax, her AGI of $115,000 reduces the $25,000 special allowance to $17,500 (phase-out at 50% of AGI over $100,000). For AMT, her adjusted AGI is $125,000 due to AMT adjustments, reducing the allowance to $12,500. Evaluate the difference in deductible rental losses between regular tax and AMT.', '["Regular tax and AMT both allow $17,500 because the allowance is not recomputed for AMT", "Regular tax allows $17,500 and AMT allows $12,500, creating a $5,000 positive AMT adjustment", "AMT disallows all passive rental losses regardless of active participation status", "AMT allows the full $40,000 loss because AMT does not apply passive activity limitations"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 58(a), the $25,000 rental loss allowance under Section 469(i) must be recomputed for AMT using AMT-adjusted AGI. Because AMT adjustments increase Dunn''s AGI from $115,000 to $125,000, the phase-out is larger, reducing the allowance from $17,500 to $12,500. The $5,000 difference is an AMT adjustment. Choice C is incorrect because passive activity rules apply for AMT; only the underlying income figures change.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q13: easy / L2 — Charitable contributions: no AMT adjustment
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Patterson, a single filer, donated $30,000 to qualified charities in 2026 and claimed the full deduction on Schedule A. For AMT purposes, which treatment applies to Patterson''s charitable contribution deduction?', '["The deduction is reduced by 25% for AMT purposes", "The deduction is entirely disallowed and added back to compute AMTI", "The deduction is allowed in full for both regular tax and AMT", "Only contributions to public charities are deductible for AMT"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Sections 56 and 57, charitable contributions are not listed as an AMT adjustment or preference item. The deduction is allowed in full for both regular tax and AMT purposes. Choice B is incorrect because charitable contributions are one of the itemized deductions that survive the AMT computation, unlike state and local taxes which are added back entirely under Section 56(b)(1)(A).', 'easy', 2),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — MTC carryforward period
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Under IRC Section 53, for how long may an individual carry forward an unused minimum tax credit?', '["Five years from the year the AMT was paid", "Ten years from the year the AMT was paid", "Twenty years from the year the AMT was paid", "Indefinitely, until the credit is fully utilized"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 53(b), the minimum tax credit may be carried forward indefinitely with no expiration date. This allows taxpayers to recover prior AMT payments whenever their regular tax exceeds the tentative minimum tax in future years. Choice B (ten years) is incorrect because it applies the carryforward period for foreign tax credits, whereas the MTC has no time limitation under Section 53.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — Completed contract method AMT adjustment
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Ridgewell Construction uses the completed contract method for a long-term contract under regular tax. For AMT, the percentage-of-completion method is required. The contract is 60% complete in 2026 with a $2,000,000 total price and $1,400,000 in estimated costs. Regular tax reports $0 income. Analyze the resulting AMT adjustment.', '["No AMT adjustment because long-term contracts are exempt from AMT rules", "$840,000 positive adjustment, representing 60% of total costs incurred", "$600,000 positive adjustment, representing the total expected profit on the contract", "$360,000 positive adjustment, representing PCM income that CCM has not yet recognized"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 56(a)(3), taxpayers using the completed contract method must use the percentage-of-completion method for AMT. PCM income = 60% × ($2,000,000 − $1,400,000) = $360,000. Since CCM reports $0, the AMT adjustment is $360,000. Choice C ($600,000) is incorrect because it treats the total expected profit as the adjustment, whereas only the 60% completion portion is recognized under PCM.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Qualified housing interest for AMT
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Okonkwo purchased his home in 2026 with a $400,000 mortgage. He also took a $50,000 home equity loan to pay off credit card debt. Annual interest is $22,000 on the mortgage and $3,500 on the home equity loan. For AMT purposes, what total mortgage interest is deductible?', '["$25,500 (both the acquisition mortgage and home equity loan interest)", "$22,000 (only the acquisition mortgage interest)", "$0 (all mortgage interest is disallowed for AMT)", "$3,500 (only the home equity loan interest)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 56(b)(1)(C), only qualified housing interest on acquisition indebtedness is deductible for AMT purposes. The $400,000 mortgage to purchase the home qualifies as acquisition debt, so the $22,000 interest is allowed. The $50,000 home equity loan used for personal credit card debt is not acquisition indebtedness. Choice A is incorrect because it includes interest on non-acquisition home equity debt, which is disallowed for AMT.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — NQSOs vs ISOs for AMT treatment
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Chen exercised nonqualified stock options with a bargain element of $80,000 and incentive stock options with a spread of $50,000 in the same year, holding the ISO shares. For regular tax, the NQSO bargain element is included in W-2 wages. Analyze which stock option exercise creates an AMT adjustment.', '["Only the ISO exercise creates an AMT adjustment because NQSOs are already taxed as ordinary income for regular tax", "Both exercises create AMT adjustments totaling $130,000", "Only the NQSO exercise creates an AMT adjustment because it involves the larger amount", "Neither exercise creates an AMT adjustment because stock option exercises are excluded from AMT"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 56(b)(3), the ISO bargain element ($50,000) is an AMT adjustment because it is not recognized for regular tax until shares are sold. NQSOs are taxed as ordinary income upon exercise under Section 83, creating no timing difference between regular tax and AMT. Choice B is incorrect because NQSOs produce no AMT timing difference, unlike ISOs where the spread creates a gap between regular tax and AMT recognition.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: hard / L4 — Evaluate TCJA SALT cap interaction with AMT
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Brennan, a high-income single filer in a high-tax state, paid $35,000 in state income taxes in 2026 but is limited to a $10,000 SALT deduction under the TCJA cap. Before TCJA, the full $35,000 would have been deducted for regular tax and then added back for AMT. Evaluate how the TCJA SALT cap affects the AMT computation compared to pre-TCJA law.', '["The AMT computation is unchanged because SALT is fully added back regardless of the TCJA cap", "AMT increases because the SALT cap reduces regular tax more than it reduces AMT", "The SALT cap reduces the AMT adjustment from $35,000 to $10,000 because only the deduction actually claimed is added back, narrowing the gap between regular and AMT", "The TCJA eliminated the AMT entirely for taxpayers in high-tax states"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 56(b)(1)(A), only the SALT deduction actually claimed on the regular return is added back for AMT. Pre-TCJA, Brennan would deduct and add back $35,000. Under TCJA, only $10,000 is deducted and added back, reducing the AMT adjustment by $25,000. This narrowing is why TCJA reduced the number of AMT-affected taxpayers. Choice A is incorrect because the AMT add-back equals the deduction claimed, not the amount of taxes paid.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Nonrefundable personal credits and AMT interaction
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Hoffman has regular tax of $10,000 before credits and a tentative minimum tax of $12,000 for 2026. She has $3,000 in nonrefundable personal credits. Analyze how AMT affects Hoffman''s total tax liability.', '["Total tax is $7,000 because credits reduce regular tax to $7,000 with no AMT", "Total tax is $12,000 because nonrefundable credits are disallowed when AMT applies", "Total tax is $10,000 because AMT replaces the regular tax computation entirely", "Total tax is $9,000 because AMT of $2,000 is added to regular tax, and the $3,000 in credits offsets the combined amount"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 55(a), AMT equals TMT minus regular tax: $12,000 − $10,000 = $2,000. Total tax before credits is $12,000. Under Section 26(a), nonrefundable personal credits can offset both regular tax and AMT, so the $3,000 credit reduces total tax to $9,000. Choice A is incorrect because it ignores the $2,000 AMT that applies since TMT ($12,000) exceeds regular tax ($10,000).', 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: medium / L2 — Tax benefit rule: state tax refund and AMT
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Patel deducted $10,000 in state income taxes on her 2025 regular return (under the SALT cap) but added the $10,000 back for AMT in 2025. In 2026, Patel receives a $2,000 state tax refund. Determine the AMT treatment of the refund in 2026.', '["The $2,000 refund is included in both regular taxable income and AMTI", "The $2,000 refund is excluded from both regular taxable income and AMTI", "The $2,000 refund is included in regular taxable income but excluded from AMTI under the tax benefit rule", "The $2,000 refund must be added to the prior year''s AMTI through an amended return"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under the tax benefit rule (IRC Section 111), a recovery is included in income only to the extent the original deduction provided a tax benefit. Because the $10,000 SALT was entirely added back for AMT in 2025, it produced no AMT benefit. The $2,000 refund is excluded from AMTI in 2026, though included in regular taxable income. Choice A is incorrect because the SALT deduction did not reduce AMT in the prior year, so no AMT recovery is required.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q21: medium / L2 — AMT for trusts and estates: lower exemption
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'The Caldwell Family Irrevocable Trust reports AMTI of $100,000 before the exemption for 2026. Trusts and estates receive an AMT exemption of $29,900. What is the trust''s AMTI subject to AMT rates?', '["$100,000 because trusts are not eligible for any AMT exemption", "$11,900 after applying the individual exemption of $88,100", "$0 because the trust''s income is below the exemption phase-out threshold", "$70,100 after subtracting the $29,900 trust exemption"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 55(d)(1)(D), trusts and estates receive a separate AMT exemption substantially lower than the individual exemption. The trust''s AMTI subject to AMT rates is $100,000 − $29,900 = $70,100. Choice B is incorrect because it applies the $88,100 single-filer exemption, whereas trusts receive a separate, much lower exemption that makes them more susceptible to AMT.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — When AMT is owed
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Under IRC Section 55, when does a taxpayer owe the alternative minimum tax?', '["When the tentative minimum tax exceeds the regular tax liability", "When taxable income exceeds a specified threshold amount", "When total itemized deductions exceed the standard deduction by more than 50%", "When the taxpayer claims more than three nonrefundable tax credits"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 55(a), AMT equals the excess of the tentative minimum tax over the regular tax liability. If TMT does not exceed regular tax, no AMT is owed. The AMT is a parallel computation ensuring taxpayers pay at least the tentative minimum amount. Choice B is incorrect because high income alone does not trigger AMT; it depends on the relationship between TMT and regular tax after adjustments and preferences.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: medium / L2 — AMTI calculation with multiple adjustments
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Vasquez, a single filer, reports regular taxable income of $320,000 for 2026. She also has $10,000 in SALT deductions claimed, $6,000 of tax-exempt private activity bond interest excluded from regular income, and exercises ISOs with a $39,000 spread. Calculate Vasquez''s AMTI before the exemption.', '["$359,000", "$375,000", "$365,000", "$369,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Sections 55–58, AMTI is computed by adding AMT adjustments and preferences to regular taxable income. Vasquez adds: $10,000 SALT (Section 56(b)(1)(A)), $6,000 private activity bond interest (Section 57(a)(5)), and $39,000 ISO spread (Section 56(b)(3)), yielding $320,000 + $55,000 = $375,000. Choice C ($365,000) is incorrect because it omits the SALT add-back, whereas all three items must be added to regular taxable income.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q24: medium / L2 — TMT calculation with two-tier rates
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Larsen, a single filer, has AMTI of $450,000 for 2026. The AMT exemption is $88,100. The 26% rate applies to the first $232,600 above the exemption, and the 28% rate applies to amounts above that threshold. Calculate Larsen''s tentative minimum tax.', '["$94,094", "$60,476", "$96,680", "$101,332"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 55(b), AMTI after exemption is $450,000 − $88,100 = $361,900. The first $232,600 is taxed at 26% ($60,476), and the remaining $129,300 at 28% ($36,204), yielding TMT of $96,680. Choice A ($94,094) is incorrect because it applies 26% to the entire $361,900, whereas the two-tier structure requires the 28% rate on amounts above $232,600.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q25: easy / L1 — Percentage depletion as preference item
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Under IRC Section 57, which of the following is classified as a tax preference item for AMT purposes?', '["Charitable contributions to qualified organizations", "Percentage depletion claimed in excess of the adjusted basis of the property", "Interest expense on primary residence acquisition indebtedness", "Qualified business income deduction under Section 199A"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 57(a)(1), percentage depletion in excess of the adjusted basis of the property is a tax preference item added to taxable income for AMTI. Unlike adjustments, preference items always increase AMTI. Choice A is incorrect because charitable contributions are not an AMT preference item or adjustment; they are allowed in full under both regular tax and AMT.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q26: medium / L3 — Disqualifying disposition eliminates AMT adjustment
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Reeves exercised ISOs on March 1, 2026, acquiring shares with a $40,000 spread. She sold the shares on September 15, 2026, before meeting the required holding periods. Analyze the AMT consequences of Reeves''s disqualifying disposition.', '["The $40,000 spread remains an AMT preference item regardless of the subsequent sale", "The disqualifying disposition eliminates the AMT adjustment because the spread is taxed as ordinary income for regular tax", "The AMT adjustment is reduced to half the spread due to the partial holding period", "The sale triggers an additional $40,000 AMT adjustment on top of the exercise adjustment"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 56(b)(3), the AMT adjustment for ISOs exists because of the timing difference between regular tax and AMT recognition. When shares are sold in a disqualifying disposition, the bargain element is taxed as ordinary income for regular tax, collapsing the timing difference. The AMT adjustment is eliminated. Choice A is incorrect because the adjustment is solely a timing item; a disqualifying disposition removes the difference between the two systems.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — AMT and NIIT are independent taxes
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'What is the relationship between the alternative minimum tax and the 3.8% net investment income tax under current law?', '["They are computed independently, and both may apply to the same taxpayer in the same year", "The NIIT replaces the AMT for taxpayers with investment income above threshold amounts", "The AMT and NIIT are combined into a single surtax computation on Form 6251", "The NIIT applies only when the taxpayer does not owe any AMT"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 55 (AMT) and Section 1411 (NIIT), these are separate, independent tax computations. A taxpayer may owe both AMT and the 3.8% NIIT in the same year because they apply to different tax bases with different threshold amounts. Choice B is incorrect because the NIIT was enacted alongside the AMT as an additional tax, not as a replacement for it.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — Excess intangible drilling costs as preference item
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'For individual AMT purposes, which of the following is a tax preference item under IRC Section 57?', '["Accelerated depreciation on residential rental property placed in service after 1998", "State and local income tax deductions claimed on Schedule A", "Excess intangible drilling costs over 65% of net oil and gas income", "Home mortgage interest on acquisition indebtedness"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 57(a)(2), excess intangible drilling costs (the amount by which IDCs exceed 65% of net income from oil and gas properties) are a tax preference item added to AMTI. Choice B (SALT) is an AMT adjustment under Section 56(b)(1)(A), not a preference item. The distinction matters because adjustments can increase or decrease AMTI, while preferences always increase it.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q29: hard / L3 — Capital gains rate cap within AMT computation
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Mercer, a single filer, has AMTI of $500,000 after the exemption for 2026. Of this amount, $180,000 consists of net long-term capital gains eligible for the 15% preferential rate. The 26% AMT rate applies to the first $232,600 and 28% to the excess. Analyze how the capital gains rate cap interacts with the AMT computation.', '["The $180,000 in capital gains is taxed at 15% within the AMT, and only the remaining $320,000 of ordinary AMTI is subject to the 26%/28% rates", "The full $500,000 is subject to the 26%/28% AMT rates because preferential capital gains rates do not apply in the AMT system", "The capital gains are excluded from AMTI entirely, reducing the AMT base to $320,000", "The capital gains are taxed at 20% under AMT rather than the regular 15% rate"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 55(b)(3), the AMT computation preserves preferential capital gains rates. Mercer''s $180,000 in long-term gains is taxed at 15% ($27,000), while the remaining $320,000 of ordinary AMTI is subject to the 26%/28% rate structure. Choice B is incorrect because Congress specifically provided that capital gains retain preferential rates within the AMT to avoid overtaxing investment income.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q30: medium / L2 — Comparing standard deduction vs itemizer AMTI
(3, 'Alternative Minimum Tax', 'Rivera claims the $15,000 standard deduction with taxable income of $180,000. Shaw, also single with $180,000 taxable income, itemizes with $10,000 SALT and $5,000 in charitable contributions. For AMT purposes, compute each taxpayer''s AMTI before the exemption.', '["Rivera: $180,000; Shaw: $180,000", "Rivera: $195,000; Shaw: $195,000", "Rivera: $180,000; Shaw: $190,000", "Rivera: $195,000; Shaw: $190,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 56, the standard deduction is added back for AMT, increasing Rivera''s AMTI to $180,000 + $15,000 = $195,000. For Shaw, only the $10,000 SALT is added back under Section 56(b)(1)(A); the $5,000 charitable deduction is allowed for AMT. Shaw''s AMTI is $190,000. Choice A is incorrect because both taxpayers have deductions that must be added back for AMT, whereas the type and amount of the add-back differs between standard deduction and itemizers.', 'medium', 2)
;

COMMIT;
